1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. Shared context and isolated context sound similar, but they're completely

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
different territory for a subagent. Assume the wrong one and your subagents fail in confusing

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
ways. This lesson maps what a subagent actually does, what it never inherits, and why that

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
isolation is a feature, not a bug. Let's dig in. Start with three basics that define isolation.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
First, every subagent runs in its own fresh conversation. Its context window is fully

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
separate from the manager's, a clean slate rather than a copy. Second, it doesn't inherit history.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The manager's conversation, its earlier tool results, and its system prompt don't carry over.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The subagent gets its own prompt and whatever you explicitly pass and nothing else. Third,

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
only the final message returns. Everything the subagent does along the way, its reasoning and

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
its tool calls, stays inside its context. The manager receives just the final result as the

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
tool output. Hold on to those three basics because every practical rule in this lesson follows from

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
them. Why is each subagent walled off like this? Because isolation is exactly what you're buying

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
when you delegate. Think about what happens without it. If every subagent's full transcript

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
flowed back into the manager, the manager's context would balloon with detail it never needs

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
and it would fill up fast. Isolation keeps the manager's context clean and focused on the goal.

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
It also bounds token growth. You can add a fifth or a tenth subtask and the manager only pays for

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
each short summary that comes back, not the entire back and forth inside every subagent.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And each subagent, seeing only its own slice, can focus hard on one problem instead of getting

19
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
distracted by everything else in flight. Let's be concrete about what a subagent actually has

20
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
to work with. It starts from a known limited set of inputs. It inherits its own system prompt,

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
the project, Claude.md file, and the tool definitions you gave it. It also receives

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
anything you explicitly passed into the delegated task, the specific data and instructions for this

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
piece of work. What it never sees is just as important. It doesn't get the manager's conversation

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,000
history, the manager's earlier tool results, or the manager's system prompt. Here's the practical

25
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
rule that falls out of this. If a subagent needs a fact to do its job, you have to hand that fact

26
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:42,000
over. Assuming it already knows is the isolation bug to watch for. Because context is isolated,

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
the delegated task is your one channel to set a subagent up to succeed. Four things belong in it.

28
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Task instructions come first. State exactly what the subagent should do and what a finished result

29
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:04,000
looks like. Next, relevant data. Hand over the specific facts, files, or values the subtask

30
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
depends on, since the subagent can't pull them from the manager. Third, output shape. Name the

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,000
format you want back, whether that's a summary, a list, or structured data. And fourth, scoped tools.

32
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Give it only the tools this task needs and nothing that widens its reach. Get these four right and

33
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
the subagent has everything it needs on the first try. This comparison captures that same isolation

34
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,000
bug in one picture. The wrong assumption is that a subagent shares the manager's full context, that

35
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
it can see everything the manager has already seen and done. Developers reach for a subagent,

36
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
then write a task that quietly relies on shared knowledge. The actual behavior is narrower. A

37
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
subagent sees only its own prompt and whatever inputs you pass into the task. Shared context

38
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
is the exception you deliberately build by passing things in, not a default you get for free. So

39
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
whenever you delegate, ask one question. Does this subagent already have, in its own inputs, everything

40
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:21,000
it needs? If not, the assumption is doing the work and it will break. Let's put a cost lens on isolation

41
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
because this is where it pays off for real builds. When a subagent finishes, the only thing that lands

42
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
in the manager's context is its final summary. Everything in between, the long reasoning, the noisy

43
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:42,000
tool output, the retries, all of that stays inside the subagent and never reaches the parent window.

44
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:50,000
That has a direct consequence for scaling. As you add more subtasks, the manager's context grows

45
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:57,000
predictably. One small summary at a time instead of exploding. One subagent can chew through a huge

46
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:03,000
document or a messy tool loop and the manager barely feels it because only the distilled result

47
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:10,000
comes back. Without isolation, that same work would flood the manager and blow your token budget.

48
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Isolation isn't only about context. It should extend to authority and that gives you three

49
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:23,000
boundaries. Least privilege comes first. A subagent holds only the permissions its task requires,

50
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
so it can read or write exactly what it needs and nothing else. Second, keep side effects limited.

51
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
A well-scoped subagent produces no writes and no actions outside its assigned subtask.

52
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
If your summarizer can send email, the scope is too wide. Third, you get a contained blast radius.

53
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
When a subagent misbehaves, and eventually one will, its narrow scope and small tool set

54
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
keep the damage local and easy to trace. Design authority the same way you design context,

55
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
hand over the minimum, and no more. Let's name the trap directly because it's the one that costs teams

56
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
the most. The common misread is treating subagents as nothing more than a speed trick, a way to run

57
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
several things at once. Parallel speed is real and it's genuinely useful, but it's the smaller

58
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:19,000
benefit by far. The deeper reason to delegate is context isolation, keeping intermediate work out

59
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
of the manager's window. Here's the tell that makes the point. Reaching for a subagent pays

60
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:31,000
off even when you only spin up one, run sequentially, purely to keep that subtask's context out of the

61
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:38,000
parent. If you only ever think in terms of parallel speed, you'll miss half of what subagents are for

62
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:45,000
and you'll skip them exactly when they'd help most. Here's the bottom line. Context isolation

63
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:51,000
gives you a clean frame for delegation. Each subagent starts fresh, so anything it needs has

64
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:58,000
to be passed in. Just remember, that isolation is the reason to delegate, not a limitation, since it

65
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:04,000
bounds token growth and contains authority. And that wraps up context isolation with scoped agents.

66
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
I'll see you in the next one.

